24
00:00:51,755 --> 00:00:53,790
What was in those boxes is the closest

25
00:00:53,810 --> 00:00:55,519
thing we have to the full story.

26
00:00:56,089 --> 00:00:57,362
Let's open the file.

27
00:01:03,689 --> 00:01:04,925
It started with a memo.

28
00:01:05,545 --> 00:01:08,371
On April 3, 1953, Richard Helms, then a

29
00:01:08,391 --> 00:01:10,852
senior officer in the CIA's covert

30
00:01:10,872 --> 00:01:13,916
operations directorate, sent a proposal to

31
00:01:13,936 --> 00:01:15,522
Director Allen Dulles.

32
00:01:15,992 --> 00:01:17,926
Ten days later, Dulles approved it.

33
00:01:18,496 --> 00:01:20,901
The goal, in the proposal's words, was

34
00:01:20,921 --> 00:01:23,389
research to develop a capability in the

35
00:01:23,409 --> 00:01:25,750
covert use of biological and chemical

36
00:01:25,770 --> 00:01:26,388
materials.

37
00:01:26,958 --> 00:01:29,695
The CIA later called MKUltra an umbrella

38
00:01:29,715 --> 00:01:30,247
project.

39
00:01:30,717 --> 00:01:32,895
Grants to researchers went out under the

40
00:01:32,915 --> 00:01:35,093
cover of research foundations, which hid

41
00:01:35,113 --> 00:01:36,741
the agency's interest from the

42
00:01:36,761 --> 00:01:38,390
researchers' own institutions.

43
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,140
From the start, normal rules were waived.

44
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:43,818
Six percent of the research budget of the

45
00:01:43,838 --> 00:01:46,119
agency's technical branch could be spent

46
00:01:46,139 --> 00:01:47,558
without formal contracts.

47
00:01:48,028 --> 00:01:50,008
Contracts, Helms noted, would reveal

48
00:01:50,028 --> 00:01:51,120
government interest.

49
00:01:51,689 --> 00:01:53,822
Researchers in this field, he wrote, are

50
00:01:53,842 --> 00:01:55,706
most reluctant to enter into signed

51
00:01:55,726 --> 00:01:57,913
agreements of any sort which connect them

52
00:01:57,933 --> 00:01:58,935
with this activity.

53
00:01:59,506 --> 00:02:01,983
Ten years later, the CIA's own Inspector

54
00:02:02,003 --> 00:02:03,856
General summed up the problem.

55
00:02:04,439 --> 00:02:06,743
The concepts involved in manipulating

56
00:02:06,763 --> 00:02:09,131
human behavior, he wrote, are found by

57
00:02:09,151 --> 00:02:11,581
many people both within and outside the

58
00:02:11,601 --> 00:02:14,032
Agency to be distasteful and unethical.

59
00:02:14,852 --> 00:02:17,069
So what did MKUltra actually do?

60
00:02:17,689 --> 00:02:20,158
The Senate's investigators described three

61
00:02:20,178 --> 00:02:20,572
phases.

62
00:02:20,912 --> 00:02:22,786
First, the search for materials worth

63
00:02:22,806 --> 00:02:23,246
studying.

64
00:02:23,587 --> 00:02:25,974
Second, laboratory tests on volunteers.

65
00:02:26,445 --> 00:02:28,670
Third, using the materials in normal life

66
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:29,162
settings.

67
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:31,532
The search ran through standing

68
00:02:31,552 --> 00:02:33,409
arrangements with specialists at

69
00:02:33,429 --> 00:02:35,522
universities, pharmaceutical houses,

70
00:02:35,542 --> 00:02:37,341
hospitals, and private research

71
00:02:37,361 --> 00:02:38,162
organizations.

72
00:02:38,733 --> 00:02:41,017
One of the first studies ran at a federal

73
00:02:41,037 --> 00:02:43,265
center in Lexington, Kentucky, which was

74
00:02:43,285 --> 00:02:45,401
then a prison for drug addicts serving

75
00:02:45,421 --> 00:02:45,963
sentences.

76
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:48,812
According to the Senate report, volunteer

77
00:02:48,832 --> 00:02:51,269
prisoners were given hallucinogenic drugs.

78
00:02:51,609 --> 00:02:53,540
As a reward, they were provided with the

79
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:54,711
drug of their addiction.

80
00:02:55,281 --> 00:02:57,695
LSD was one of the materials tested.

81
00:02:58,115 --> 00:02:59,859
And the last phase of testing went

82
00:02:59,879 --> 00:03:00,274
further.

83
00:03:00,856 --> 00:03:03,139
Undercover officers of the Bureau of

84
00:03:03,159 --> 00:03:05,762
Narcotics, acting for the CIA, gave it to

85
00:03:05,782 --> 00:03:08,385
unwitting people in normal life settings.

86
00:03:08,805 --> 00:03:11,065
People who had never agreed to take part.

87
00:03:11,635 --> 00:03:13,676
The Senate report quotes the rationale.

88
00:03:14,016 --> 00:03:16,001
Testing under accepted scientific

89
00:03:16,021 --> 00:03:18,554
procedures, it said, fails to disclose the

90
00:03:18,574 --> 00:03:21,106
full pattern of reactions and attributions

91
00:03:21,126 --> 00:03:23,598
that may occur in operational situations.

92
00:03:24,168 --> 00:03:26,432
And a CIA passage quoted in the same

93
00:03:26,452 --> 00:03:28,716
report says the effectiveness of the

94
00:03:28,736 --> 00:03:31,190
substances on individuals at all social

95
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:33,791
levels, high and low, native American and

96
00:03:33,811 --> 00:03:35,948
foreign, is of great significance.

97
00:03:36,518 --> 00:03:39,319
In 1977, the CIA told the Senate that some

98
00:03:39,339 --> 00:03:41,872
of the unwitting testing took place in

99
00:03:41,892 --> 00:03:44,559
safehouses in San Francisco and New York

100
00:03:44,579 --> 00:03:44,895
City.

101
00:03:45,465 --> 00:03:47,630
The Inspector General's report also

102
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:49,939
explains why so little of this can be

103
00:03:49,959 --> 00:03:50,813
reconstructed.

104
00:03:51,395 --> 00:03:53,756
Present practice, it says, is to maintain

105
00:03:53,776 --> 00:03:56,196
no records of the planning and approval of

106
00:03:56,216 --> 00:03:57,009
test programs.

107
00:03:57,579 --> 00:03:59,766
According to the same report, the people

108
00:03:59,786 --> 00:04:01,972
tested were seldom accessible beyond the

109
00:04:01,992 --> 00:04:03,295
first hours of the test.

110
00:04:03,635 --> 00:04:06,081
In a number of cases, a subject became ill

111
00:04:06,101 --> 00:04:07,137
for hours or days.

112
00:04:08,057 --> 00:04:10,516
One test is documented in more detail than

113
00:04:10,536 --> 00:04:11,106
any other.

114
00:04:11,726 --> 00:04:14,110
On November 18, 1953, a group of ten

115
00:04:14,130 --> 00:04:16,647
scientists from the CIA and the Army's

116
00:04:16,667 --> 00:04:19,051
Camp Detrick, in Maryland, met for a

117
00:04:19,071 --> 00:04:21,855
review conference at a cabin at Deep Creek

118
00:04:21,875 --> 00:04:22,188
Lake.

119
00:04:22,759 --> 00:04:25,234
One of them was Frank Olson, a civilian

120
00:04:25,254 --> 00:04:27,664
employee of the Army, and an expert in

121
00:04:27,684 --> 00:04:28,432
aerobiology.

122
00:04:29,002 --> 00:04:31,444
After dinner on November 19, the group was

123
00:04:31,464 --> 00:04:33,144
served a bottle of Cointreau.

124
00:04:33,484 --> 00:04:36,175
A CIA officer, Robert Lashbrook, had put

125
00:04:36,195 --> 00:04:36,853
LSD in it.

126
00:04:37,193 --> 00:04:39,688
Olson received about 70 micrograms.

127
00:04:40,158 --> 00:04:42,233
About twenty minutes later, according to

128
00:04:42,253 --> 00:04:44,223
Gottlieb's account, he told the others

129
00:04:44,243 --> 00:04:45,271
what they had taken.

130
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:48,142
The Senate report found that neither the

131
00:04:48,162 --> 00:04:50,290
chief of the CIA's Technical Services

132
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:52,727
Staff, where Gottlieb worked, nor the head

133
00:04:52,747 --> 00:04:55,107
of the covert operations directorate, had

134
00:04:55,127 --> 00:04:57,428
